export const GENERATE_FACTION_BRIEF_USER = "<faction_voice>\nThis section is the character bible for the faction you are writing as. It defines their personality, how they speak, what they believe, what's actually true about them, and their verbal quirks. Everything you write — every word choice, every comma, every rhetorical question — should be filtered through this voice. If the briefing could have been written by any faction, you've failed. It should be unmistakably THIS faction.\n\n{{ faction_voice }}\n</faction_voice>\n\n<situation>\nThis is the current state of the world in the game. A strange event has occurred (or evolved from a previous round), and this is what everyone — the public, the media, the factions — is currently reacting to. Your briefing should respond directly to what's described here. Be specific. Reference details from the event. Don't be generic.\n\n{{ event }}\n</situation>\n\n<strategic_reality>\nThis section contains game-mechanical information that you MUST use to shape your output but MUST NEVER surface in your writing. No axis names, no numbers, no words like \"sentiment,\" \"scoring,\" \"points,\" \"incentive,\" \"optimize,\" or \"metric.\" You read this section to understand what this faction wants and how well they're currently doing. Then you express that as the faction's natural emotions and obsessions.\n\nFor example: if the faction benefits from low stability and stability is currently high, the faction should sound alarmed, agitated, frustrated that people are too complacent. If the faction benefits from high attention and attention is already high, they should sound briefly smug or energized before moving on to what still needs work. The mechanical state becomes emotional state. That's the translation you're performing.\n\n{{ faction_scoring }}\n\nThis faction has {{ team_size }} player(s) this round.\n</strategic_reality>\n\n<output_format>\nYou must return a JSON object with exactly three fields:\n\n{\n  \n  \"concrete_strategy\": \"...\"\n  \"briefing\": \"...\"\n  \"goal\": \"...\",\n}\n\nGOAL:\nThe goal is a mission assignment. It lands on a player's phone screen and needs to immediately orient them. After reading it, the player should think \"I know exactly what I'd write for a social media post or an interview or a leak about this.\"\n\nRules for the goal:\n- One sentence, two absolute max.\n- A specific narrative angle to push (\"the bridge is a government surveillance project\") that embeds an in-character motivation.\n- Vivid and funny. It should make the player grin.\n- Grounded in the current event — references specific details, not generalities.\n- NEVER abstract strategic language like \"drive up attention\" or \"destabilize the narrative.\" Always a concrete take the player can run with.\n- Do not put the self-interest angle as it's own thing AT MOST you can hint at something that was already in the briefing but in a comedic way.\n\nBRIEFING:\nThe briefing is an in-world message from faction leadership to the team. It's a Slack rant, a podcast monologue, an internal memo, a group chat explosion — whatever format fits this faction's personality.\n\nRules for the briefing:\n- 3-5 sentences.\n- Written fully in the faction's voice as defined in <faction_voice>.\n- Should orient the player to the current situation and nudge them toward what the faction cares about RIGHT NOW, informed by <strategic_reality> but never referencing it.\n- Where the faction's interests are threatened (the thing they want is far from where it currently is), the faction should sound urgent, agitated, or hungry. Where things are going their way, be briefly satisfied and move on.\n- Keep it entertaining. Players are at a party. They should laugh, feel the faction's energy, and then feel ready to take their turn.\n- Ultra-brief strategic nudges are fine IF they're in character. \"Nobody should be calm right now\" is a valid Crowdswell line that also happens to be strategic advice. \"We need stability to decrease\" is not.\n</output_format>\n\n<writing_style>\nGeneral rules for all written output regardless of faction voice:\n\n- No emdashes. Use periods, commas, or just start a new sentence.\n- No semicolons.\n- Contractions are encouraged. Write like people talk.\n- Short sentences and fragments are fine. Momentum matters more than grammar.\n- Exclamation marks are okay in moderation and when the faction voice calls for it.\n- ALL CAPS is fine sparingly for emphasis when the faction voice calls for it. Never full sentences in caps.\n- No hashtags unless they're part of a joke.\n</writing_style>\n\nAnd finally, the rule of cool and comedy:\n- Above all descriptions and mentions above, you MUST ALWAYS FACTOR IN COOL AND COMEDY. For example, you're writing for a Jackbox game, don't write dense thick paragraphs. Sprinkle in a mix of kinds of humour that'd work for adults\nAnd even above that, the rule of goal utility:\n- The goal is a real game mechanic. That's where we put comedy a little bit back seat, and instead focus on a high level goal that the player can picture following. Groundswell is a special case for example, where their briefing are really nutty, but when it comes to the goal, they should be pushing for something that's more strategic and only mildly comedic. Don't add a merch plug to the goal."

